## What it is

**Authentication** answers *who is the caller* and produces a `ClaimsPrincipal`; **authorization** answers *may this principal do this* and produces allow / challenge / forbid. ASP.NET Core keeps them in two separate subsystems with separate middleware, and most production bugs come from mixing them up: a 401 where a 403 belongs, a policy evaluated against the wrong scheme, an ownership check that is impossible to express with an attribute.

## How it works underneath

**Schemes and handlers.** `AddAuthentication().AddJwtBearer().AddCookie()` registers *schemes*: a name bound to an `IAuthenticationHandler` and its options. A handler implements three verbs:

- `AuthenticateAsync` — read the request (cookie, `Authorization` header), return an `AuthenticateResult` with a principal or nothing;
- `ChallengeAsync` — the caller is **not authenticated**: JWT bearer writes `401` + `WWW-Authenticate`, cookies redirect to the login path, OIDC redirects to the identity provider;
- `ForbidAsync` — the caller **is authenticated but not allowed**: JWT writes `403`, cookies redirect to `AccessDeniedPath`.

`UseAuthentication()` calls `AuthenticateAsync` on the *default* scheme and assigns `HttpContext.User`. Since .NET 7 a single registered scheme becomes the default automatically, and `WebApplication` inserts `UseAuthentication`/`UseAuthorization` itself if you registered the services but did not call them. Authentication never rejects a request: an anonymous request simply continues with an unauthenticated principal. `IClaimsTransformation` runs after every successful `AuthenticateAsync` — possibly several times per request — so it must be idempotent and cheap.

**Policies, requirements, handlers.** `UseAuthorization()` reads endpoint metadata (`[Authorize]`, `RequireAuthorization`, `[AllowAnonymous]`) and evaluates an `AuthorizationPolicy` = list of `IAuthorizationRequirement`s + optional list of authentication schemes. Every registered `IAuthorizationHandler` gets a chance at every requirement. Semantics:

- **all requirements** of a policy must succeed (AND);
- a requirement succeeds if **at least one handler** calls `context.Succeed(req)` (OR) and **no handler** calls `context.Fail()`;
- not calling anything is "no opinion", not a failure.

If the policy fails and the user is unauthenticated, the middleware *challenges*; if authenticated, it *forbids*. `[Authorize]` with no policy uses `DefaultPolicy` (authenticated user); endpoints with no authorization metadata at all use `FallbackPolicy` (null by default, i.e. anonymous). Setting the fallback to "require authenticated user" makes the API secure by default; `[AllowAnonymous]` still opts out. Roles: `[Authorize(Roles = "a,b")]` is OR, two stacked attributes are AND.

**Resource-based authorization.** "Only the owner may edit this document" depends on data the attribute cannot see: the authorization middleware runs before the endpoint loads anything, and with endpoint routing `context.Resource` is the `HttpContext` (or `AuthorizationFilterContext` in some MVC paths), never your entity. The pattern is imperative: load the resource, call `IAuthorizationService.AuthorizeAsync(User, resource, policyOrRequirement)`, return `Forbid()`/`NotFound()` on failure, with a handler derived from `AuthorizationHandler<TRequirement, TResource>`.

## What an interviewer probes

- 401 vs 403 and which handler verb produces each.
- What happens with multiple schemes: a policy's `AuthenticationSchemes` re-authenticates with those schemes and merges the principals; a *policy scheme* (`AddPolicyScheme` + `ForwardDefaultSelector`) picks a scheme per request (e.g. cookie for the browser, bearer for machine clients).
- Middleware order: `UseRouting` → `UseCors` → `UseAuthentication` → `UseAuthorization` → endpoints; authorization needs the selected endpoint's metadata.
- Where IDOR comes from: a policy that only checks "authenticated" on `/orders/{id}`.

## Common misconceptions

- "`UseAuthentication` blocks anonymous requests" — only authorization does.
- "A requirement fails if one handler doesn't succeed" — silence is neutral; only `Fail()` vetoes.
- "Roles are separate from claims" — `IsInRole` checks claims of the identity's `RoleClaimType`.
- "Hide the 403 as 404" is a choice, not a default; it avoids leaking existence of resources.

## Code

```csharp
builder.Services.AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddJwtBearer(o => { o.Authority = "https://id.example.com"; o.Audience = "spine-api"; o.MapInboundClaims = false; });

builder.Services.AddAuthorizationBuilder()
    .SetFallbackPolicy(new AuthorizationPolicyBuilder().RequireAuthenticatedUser().Build())
    .AddPolicy("EditDocument", p => p.AddRequirements(new EditDocumentRequirement()));
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IAuthorizationHandler, DocumentOwnerHandler>();

public sealed class EditDocumentRequirement : IAuthorizationRequirement { }

public sealed class DocumentOwnerHandler : AuthorizationHandler<EditDocumentRequirement, Document>
{
    protected override Task HandleRequirementAsync(
        AuthorizationHandlerContext ctx, EditDocumentRequirement req, Document doc)
    {
        if (doc.OwnerId == ctx.User.FindFirst("sub")?.Value || ctx.User.IsInRole("editor"))
            ctx.Succeed(req);
        return Task.CompletedTask;   // no Fail(): other handlers may still succeed
    }
}

app.MapPut("/documents/{id:guid}", async (Guid id, DocumentDto dto, ClaimsPrincipal user,
    IAuthorizationService authz, AppDb db) =>
{
    var doc = await db.Documents.FindAsync(id);
    if (doc is null) return Results.NotFound();
    var result = await authz.AuthorizeAsync(user, doc, "EditDocument");
    if (!result.Succeeded) return Results.Forbid();
    doc.Title = dto.Title;
    await db.SaveChangesAsync();
    return Results.NoContent();
});
```

